$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A grave charge and a thin file</h2>
<p>Few allegations are graver than trading in newborns. The prosecution&rsquo;s case was that the applicant, working as an Asha worker, used her access to vulnerable women to identify mothers, that buyers were dressed up as adoptive parents, and that infants changed hands for money. She was arrested in February 2024 and spent over two and a half years in custody before this order.</p>
<p>What the file actually contained, however, was startlingly little. The State&rsquo;s own position before the Court came down to two items: a CCTV clip said to show the applicant holding an infant, and photographs of infants on a mobile phone. The State accepted that no other admissible evidence existed against her.</p>

<h2>How each piece of evidence failed</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>The CCTV clip</strong><br>The IO sent the DVR to the FSL without retaining a copy of the footage — keeping only photographs taken of the video while it played. In those photographs, the face was not identifiable.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>The phone images</strong><br>The photographs of infants were never shown to have come from the applicant&rsquo;s phone, and the phone itself was not mirror-imaged before despatch to the FSL — breaking any authenticated chain between the device and the accused.</div>
</div>
<p>On that record, the Court held there was no reliable evidence connecting the applicant to the offence, granted bail on a personal bond of Rs 20,000 with one surety, and — unusually — directed that the order be sent to the Deputy Commissioner of Police concerned to look into how the investigation was conducted.</p>

<h2>Why this order matters beyond its facts</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Three propositions worth retaining:</strong></p>
  <p>&bull; Electronic evidence has a chain of custody like any other: a DVR despatched without preserving a working copy, or a phone sent for analysis without imaging, may leave the prosecution unable to prove its own exhibits.</p>
  <p>&bull; The gravity of an allegation cannot substitute for the quality of the material. Courts have repeatedly refused to let the label of trafficking, or any other serious offence, carry a case that the evidence does not.</p>
  <p>&bull; Judicial supervision of investigation quality is real: sending a bail order to the DCP for administrative examination signals that defective investigation has institutional consequences, not just forensic ones.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the High Court of Delhi dated 26 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the pending trial.</div>
